<section class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">CLINICAL EVIDENCE</span><h1>Research you can examine.</h1><p>Four supplied publications, with their findings and limits. Assessment evidence is kept separate from home rehabilitation claims.</p></section><div class="container section"><div class="filter-bar" aria-label="Filter research"><button class="filter selected" data-study-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All publications · 4</button><button class="filter " data-study-filter="vr" aria-pressed="false">VR assessment · 2</button><button class="filter " data-study-filter="related" aria-pressed="false">Related assessment · 2</button></div><div class="study-grid"><article class="study-card" data-study-group="vr"><div class="study-meta">VR EYE-TRACKING ASSESSMENT</div><small>npj Digital Medicine · 2024 · 7:219</small><h3>A portable and efficient dementia screening tool using eye tracking machine learning and virtual reality</h3><p class="fine">Xu Y, Zhang C, Pan B, Yuan Q, Zhang X</p><div class="study-result"><b>201 participants</b><span>MoCA correlation r = 0.90; sensitivity 88.5%; specificity 83%</span></div><p>VECA combined VR eye tracking with machine learning to estimate MoCA scores and classify cognitive impairment. The study used five-fold cross-validation.</p><details><summary>Applicability & limitations</summary><p><b>Scope:</b> VR eye-tracking assessment research; the paper includes Shenzhen Yiwei Technology affiliations. Commercial-model equivalence needs documentation.</p><p><b>Limitations:</b> Shenzhen cohort; no cross-center training/testing. Screening agreement does not establish home-training benefit, disease prevention or diagnostic certainty.</p></details><div class="buttons"><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-024-01206-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Publisher article</a><a class="text-link" href="{{ 'npj-vr-eye-tracking.pdf' | asset_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplied PDF</a></div></article><article class="study-card" data-study-group="vr"><div class="study-meta">VR EYE-TRACKING ASSESSMENT</div><small>Chinese Journal of Magnetic Resonance Imaging · 2024 · 15(6):49–53</small><h3>Application value of structural MRI combined with computerized cognitive assessment based on VR eye-tracking technology in early diagnosis of Alzheimer’s disease</h3><p class="fine">Wei Z et al.</p><div class="study-result"><b>135 participants · 77 early AD / 58 cognitively unimpaired</b><span>Combined AUC 0.825 vs hippocampal-volume-only AUC 0.629</span></div><p>A five-minute VR eye-tracking assessment was studied alongside structural MRI. Adding the assessment score improved discrimination compared with hippocampal volume alone (P < 0.001).</p><details><summary>Applicability & limitations</summary><p><b>Scope:</b> Clinical assessment plus imaging; not a home rehabilitation trial.</p><p><b>Limitations:</b> Single-site clinical sample. The combined result requires MRI and must not be attributed to the headset alone.</p></details><div class="buttons"><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.12015/issn.1674-8034.2024.06.007" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Publisher article</a><a class="text-link" href="{{ 'mri-vr-assessment.pdf' | asset_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplied PDF</a></div></article><article class="study-card" data-study-group="related"><div class="study-meta">RELATED ASSESSMENT RESEARCH</div><small>BMC Public Health · 2024 · 24:1206</small><h3>Community screening for dementia among older adults in China: a machine learning-based strategy</h3><p class="fine">Zhang Y et al.</p><div class="study-result"><b>823 adults aged 65 years and older</b><span>MLP AUC 0.83 ± 0.04 vs AD8 baseline AUC 0.65</span></div><p>Machine-learning models combined selected AD8 questions with health characteristics in the SHARE community cohort.</p><details><summary>Applicability & limitations</summary><p><b>Scope:</b> Related community-screening methodology. This study did not test a VR device or demonstrate QuickMind efficacy.</p><p><b>Limitations:</b> Performance relates to this cohort, selected health features and the study reference assessment; it is not a VR product performance claim.</p></details><div class="buttons"><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-024-18692-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Publisher article</a><a class="text-link" href="{{ 'bmc-community-screening.pdf' | asset_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplied PDF</a></div></article><article class="study-card" data-study-group="related"><div class="study-meta">RELATED ASSESSMENT RESEARCH</div><small>American Journal of Alzheimer’s Disease & Other Dementias · 2024 · 39</small><h3>Development and Validation of an Automatic Computerized Neurocognitive Battery in Chinese</h3><p class="fine">Zhang J, Hong Z, Yang L, Li X, Ye F</p><div class="study-result"><b>86 volunteers · ages 51–82</b><span>Manual/system scoring correlation 0.904–1.0</span></div><p>The CNBC provided automated testing and scoring. Correlations with paired paper-based measures ranged from 0.256 to 0.666.</p><details><summary>Applicability & limitations</summary><p><b>Scope:</b> Related computerized-assessment validation. It is not a VR rehabilitation study; supplied material does not establish QuickMind product equivalence.</p><p><b>Limitations:</b> Preliminary Chinese-speaking volunteer sample; score agreement is not treatment efficacy or diagnostic accuracy.</p></details><div class="buttons"><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1177/15333175241271910" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Publisher article</a><a class="text-link" href="{{ 'computerized-battery.pdf' | asset_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplied PDF</a></div></article></div><div class="notice">These studies investigate assessment or screening. They do not establish the treatment efficacy, prevention benefit or regulatory status of the proposed QuickMind Home Plan.</div><div class="buttons"><a class="btn outline" href="/pages/registrations" >Explore Registration Documents</a><a class="text-link" href="{{ settings.home_product.url | default: '/collections/all' }}" >Return to Home Product</a></div></div>